.TH SYSLOGCEF 1 "November 2026" "syslog2cef 0.4.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
syslogcef \- convert syslog events to ArcSight Common Event Format (CEF)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B syslogcef
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIPATHS\fR...]
.br
.B syslogcef
.BI \-\-listen " PROTO:PORT"
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B syslogcef
converts syslog events into ArcSight Common Event Format (CEF) for
consumption by SIEM platforms. It reads from files, standard input, or
the network, and writes CEF records to standard output, a file, a
remote SIEM, or a Kafka topic.
.PP
Ten input formats are detected automatically: RFC3164 (BSD, with or
without a year in the timestamp), RFC5424 (including structured data),
rsyslog JSON and file formats, systemd journal exports (JSON, short,
and ISO), native Cisco console output (sequence numbers and clock-state
markers), ISO-timestamp syslog with PRI, and key=value streams
(Fortinet FortiGate, Sophos XG). Lines matching none of these are
analyzed adaptively: a timestamp shape and host candidate are located
and a pattern for the layout is synthesized and cached. Unparseable
lines never abort the stream; they fall back to a raw-message event.
.PP
Vendor mappings for Cisco ASA/IOS, F5 BIG-IP, Fortinet, Sophos,
VMware, and generic Linux are bundled and selected automatically from
message content. Log content is treated as untrusted input: CEF header
and extension values are escaped so crafted messages cannot forge
header fields or split records.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Input
.TP
.I PATHS
Input files. When omitted and
.B \-\-listen
is not given, lines are read from standard input.
.TP
.B \-\-tail
Follow the input files like
.BR "tail \-f" ;
all given files are tailed concurrently.
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " PROTO:PORT"
Receive syslog over the network instead of reading files or standard
input. Accepts
.BR udp: \fIPORT\fR,
.BR tcp: \fIPORT\fR,
or
.IB PROTO : HOST : PORT
to bind a specific address (default: all interfaces). TCP accepts
multiple concurrent connections with newline-delimited messages.
Overrides \fIPATHS\fR and
.BR \-\-tail .
.TP
.BI \-\-mode " MODE"
Force a specific parser instead of auto-detection. One of
.BR rfc3164 ,
.BR rfc5424 ,
.BR rsyslog_json ,
.BR rsyslog_file ,
.BR journald_json ,
.BR journald_short ,
.BR journald_iso ,
.BR cisco_seq ,
.BR iso_syslog ,
.BR kv ,
or the name of a pattern loaded with
.BR \-\-patterns .
.SS Conversion
.TP
.BI \-\-mapping " FILE"
Use the mapping JSON file \fIFILE\fR. When omitted, a bundled mapping
is selected automatically from message content.
.TP
.BI \-\-patterns " FILE"
Load custom patterns from the JSON file \fIFILE\fR (repeatable; files
are used in the order given). A pattern is a named regular expression
whose named groups become event fields. A \fBline\fR pattern parses a
whole line whose header format no built\-in parser knows; a \fBmsg\fR
pattern picks fields out of the message of an already parsed event.
Captured fields are available to mapping templates as
\fB%(\fR\fIname\fR\fB)s\fR, and a pattern may name the mapping to use
for the events it matches. See
.B PATTERN FILES
below for the format. Pattern names are accepted by
.BR \-\-mode .
Files are validated at startup; an invalid regex, a reserved group
name, a bad mapping, or a duplicate name stops the program with exit
status 2 and a message naming the file and pattern. An unknown key in
a pattern or example is ignored with a warning on standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-patterns
Run the \fBexamples\fR of every pattern in every
.B \-\-patterns
file instead of converting input, and print one line per example,
.BI "PASS " "pattern " "example " N
or
.BI "FAIL " "pattern " "example " N ": " problems\fR,
followed by
.IB passed / total " examples passed" .
Each unknown key in a pattern or example (usually a typo such as
\fBexmaples\fR) is reported first as
.BI "FAIL " "pattern " "config: unknown key '" key '
and counts as a failure.
Exit status is 0 when all examples pass and no unknown keys were found
(also when no file defines any examples, which prints
\fBno examples defined\fR) and 1 otherwise. Requires at
least one
.BR \-\-patterns ;
cannot be combined with \fIPATHS\fR,
.BR \-\-listen ,
or
.BR \-\-tail .
.TP
.B \-\-validate
Check CEF extension values against the ArcSight extension dictionary
(known keys, data types, port ranges, IP/MAC/timestamp formats,
maximum string lengths). Violations are logged as warnings; conversion
continues. Unknown keys are advisory only, as the CEF standard permits
custom keys.
.TP
.B \-\-strict
Like
.BR \-\-validate ,
but exit with status 1 on the first type or length violation.
.TP
.B \-\-multiprocess
Convert lines using a process pool.
.TP
.BI \-\-pool\-size " N"
Worker count for
.B \-\-multiprocess
(default: CPU count minus one).
.SS Output
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " \fIFILE\fR"
Write CEF lines to \fIFILE\fR instead of standard output. Flushed per
line in
.B \-\-tail
and
.B \-\-listen
modes. \fIFILE\fR may contain
.BR strftime (3)
codes, for example
.IR /var/log/syslogcef/%Y\-%m\-%d/events\-%H.cef :
the file is reopened whenever the rendered path changes (hourly in this
example), parent directories are created as needed, and templated paths
are always opened in append mode. Unsupported or platform\-specific
codes are rejected at startup; write \fB%%\fR for a literal percent.
An empty \fIFILE\fR is treated as standard output.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-append
Append to the output file instead of truncating it (implied for
strftime\-templated paths). Used by the system service so restarts never
lose archived events.
.TP
.BI \-\-send " URL"
Forward each CEF record to a remote destination:
.RS
.TP
.BI udp:// HOST : PORT
one datagram per record.
.TP
.BI tcp:// HOST : PORT
newline-delimited stream with automatic reconnect and backoff.
.TP
.BI kafka:// BROKER : PORT / TOPIC
produce to a Kafka topic; requires the optional dependency
(\fBpip install syslog2cef[kafka]\fR).
.RE
.IP
Combinable with
.B \-\-output
to archive locally while forwarding, and with
.B \-\-listen
for a complete receive\-convert\-forward daemon.
.TP
.BI \-\-eps " N"
Rate-limit
.B \-\-send
to at most \fIN\fR events per second.
.TP
.B \-\-explain
Instead of bare CEF, print one block per input line to standard
output, separated by blank lines:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\-\-\- line \fIN\fR
raw:     \fIthe input line\fR
parser:  \fIparser that read the header\fR
pattern: \fIline pattern name, or \-\fR
msg pattern: \fImsg pattern name, or \-\fR
mapping: \fB\-\-mapping\fR | \fBpattern\fR \fIname\fR | \fBauto\fR \fIbundled\-name\fR
fields:
  \fIkey\fR = \fIvalue\fR
cef:     \fIthe CEF record\fR
.fi
.RE
.IP
The fields are every value mapping templates can use, except
\fBraw\fR, sorted by name; a value longer than 200 characters is
shortened, and a trailing \fB...\fR marks the cut.
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-send ,
.BR \-\-output ,
or
.BR \-\-multiprocess .
.TP
.B \-\-stats
When input ends (or on interrupt), print a summary to standard error:
the number of lines, counts by parser and by msg pattern (sorted by
count), the number of lines no parser recognized (handled by the
adaptive fallback or kept as raw\-message events), and the first five
of those lines, each truncated to 200 characters. Works with normal
output,
.BR \-\-output ,
.BR \-\-send ,
and
.BR \-\-explain ;
cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-multiprocess .
.SS General
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-level " LEVEL"
Python logging level (default
.BR WARNING ).
Validation findings are emitted at WARNING.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version, copyright, license and author, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary, then exit.
.SH PATTERN FILES
A pattern file is a JSON object with a non\-empty \fBpatterns\fR list.
Patterns are tried in file order. Each pattern accepts the keys below.
Any other key in a pattern or an example is ignored with a warning on
standard error naming the file, pattern and key, and is reported as a
failure by
.BR \-\-check\-patterns .
.TP
.B name
Required. Unique name; must not be a built\-in parser name.
.TP
.B regex
Required. A Python regular expression. Shorthands \fB%{\fR\fINAME\fR\fB}\fR
(match without capturing) and \fB%{\fR\fINAME\fR\fB:\fR\fIfield\fR\fB}\fR
(capture as \fIfield\fR) insert a predefined regex. \fINAME\fR is one
of
.BR IPV4 ,
.BR IPV6 ,
.B IP
(IPv4 or IPv6),
.BR INT ,
.BR NUMBER ,
.BR PORT ,
.BR WORD ,
.BR NOTSPACE ,
.B DATA
(lazy \fB.*?\fR),
.B GREEDYDATA
(\fB.*\fR),
.BR HOSTNAME ,
.BR USER ,
.BR MAC ,
.BR SYSLOGTIMESTAMP ,
.BR TIMESTAMP_ISO8601 ,
.BR QUOTEDSTRING ,
.BR PATH ,
or
.B LOGLEVEL
(case\-insensitive syslog level words).
.TP
.B match
\fBline\fR (default) applies the regex to the whole raw line with a
match anchored at its start. Line patterns are tried before
(\fBpriority\fR \fBbefore\fR) or after (default) the built\-in
parsers, ahead of the adaptive fallback. The groups \fBpri\fR,
\fBhost\fR, \fBapp\fR, \fBpid\fR, \fBmsgid\fR, \fBmsg\fR and
\fBts\fR fill the event header; any other named group becomes a field.
.IP
\fBmsg\fR applies the regex to the message of every successfully parsed
event (built\-in parser, line pattern, adaptive fallback, or forced
\fB\-\-mode\fR), searching anywhere in it; anchor it with \fB^\fR.
The first matching msg pattern wins. Every named group becomes a field;
the header group names above are not allowed.
.TP
.B timestamp_format
Line patterns only; required exactly when the regex has a \fBts\fR
group. A
.BR strptime (3)
format, \fBiso8601\fR, or \fBepoch\fR. Formats without a year get
the same year inference as the built\-in parsers.
.TP
.B priority
Line patterns only: \fBbefore\fR or \fBafter\fR (default).
.TP
.B app
Msg patterns only: a string or list of strings. The pattern is tried
only on events whose program name equals one of them, ignoring case.
.TP
.B mapping
A bundled mapping name
.RB ( cisco_asa ,
.BR cisco_ios ,
.BR f5 ,
.BR fortinet ,
.BR linux ,
.BR sophos ,
.BR vmware )
or a mapping JSON file, relative paths being resolved against the
pattern file's directory. Used for the events the pattern matches; a
matching msg pattern's mapping takes precedence over a line pattern's,
and
.B \-\-mapping
takes precedence over both. Validated at load time.
.TP
.B examples
A list of objects with a required \fBline\fR (a complete input line),
an optional \fBexpect\fR object of field names and expected values,
and an optional \fBmatches\fR (default true; false means the pattern
must not match the line). Run by
.BR \-\-check\-patterns .
.TP
.B description
Optional free text for people reading the file; not used by syslogcef.
.PP
These names are derived by syslogcef and cannot be used as group names
in either kind of pattern:
.BR facility ,
.BR severity ,
.BR timestamp ,
.BR ts_orig ,
.BR source_hint ,
.BR raw ,
.BR raw_kv ,
.BR rt ,
.BR message_length ,
.BR message_short ,
.BR dvcpid .
.PP
A captured field with the same name as a key=value pair in the message
replaces it, and a capture named after a known alias (for example
\fBsrc_ip\fR) also provides the canonical CEF name (\fBsrc\fR).
.PP
Example: OpenSSH logins, mapped with a mapping file that uses
\fB%(src)s\fR, \fB%(spt)s\fR, \fB%(suser)s\fR, and \fB%(outcome)s\fR:
.PP
.nf
.RS
{
  "patterns": [
    {
      "name": "sshd_auth",
      "match": "msg",
      "app": ["sshd", "sshd\-session"],
      "regex": "^(?P<outcome>Accepted|Failed) %{NOTSPACE:auth_method} for (?:invalid user )?%{USER:suser} from %{IP:src} port %{PORT:spt}",
      "mapping": "sshd\-mapping.json",
      "examples": [
        {
          "line": "<38>Nov 21 10:15:09 bastion sshd[2214]: Accepted publickey for deploy from 198.51.100.4 port 40022 ssh2",
          "expect": {"suser": "deploy", "src": "198.51.100.4", "spt": 40022}
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
.RE
.fi
.PP
Pattern regexes run against untrusted input with no time limit: anchor
them, and never nest unbounded repetition such as
.B (\ew+\es?)+
(catastrophic backtracking).
The source distribution's \fIdocs/patterns.md\fR is a complete
authoring guide.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Strict validation failed, an input file could not be read, a
network destination was unreachable after retries, or an example
failed or an unknown key was found under
.BR \-\-check\-patterns .
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line arguments, or a pattern or mapping file failed
validation.
.SH EXAMPLES
Convert a log file to CEF on standard output:
.PP
.nf
.RS
syslogcef < /var/log/syslog
.RE
.fi
.PP
Convert several files into one archive:
.PP
.nf
.RS
syslogcef /var/log/messages /var/log/secure \-o events.cef
.RE
.fi
.PP
Follow two files in real time, appending CEF to an archive:
.PP
.nf
.RS
syslogcef /var/log/asa.log /var/log/messages \-\-tail \-\-append \-o events.cef
.RE
.fi
.PP
Act as a syslog receiver and forward CEF to an ArcSight connector:
.PP
.nf
.RS
syslogcef \-\-listen udp:514 \-\-send tcp://siem.example.com:514
.RE
.fi
.PP
Receive on a high port (no privileges needed), archive locally, and
forward rate-limited to 5000 events per second:
.PP
.nf
.RS
syslogcef \-\-listen tcp:5514 \-\-append \-o /var/log/syslogcef/events.cef \\
          \-\-send udp://siem.example.com:514 \-\-eps 5000
.RE
.fi
.PP
Stream converted events into Kafka:
.PP
.nf
.RS
syslogcef /var/log/messages \-\-tail \-\-send kafka://broker.example.com:9092/cef\-events
.RE
.fi
.PP
Force the Cisco ASA mapping and validate the output strictly:
.PP
.nf
.RS
syslogcef asa.log \-\-mapping /etc/syslogcef/mapping.json \-\-strict
.RE
.fi
.PP
Develop a pattern file: see which lines no parser recognizes, inspect
one line, test the examples, then convert:
.PP
.nf
.RS
syslogcef \-\-stats /var/log/secure > /dev/null
grep sshd /var/log/secure | head \-n 3 | syslogcef \-\-explain \-\-patterns sshd.json
syslogcef \-\-patterns sshd.json \-\-check\-patterns
syslogcef \-\-patterns sshd.json /var/log/secure \-o secure.cef
.RE
.fi
.PP
High-volume batch conversion with a process pool:
.PP
.nf
.RS
syslogcef big.log \-\-multiprocess \-\-pool\-size 8 \-o big.cef
.RE
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/syslogcef/syslogcef.conf
Configuration for the system service (environment file): input file or
network endpoints, output file, and extra arguments. To run the
service as a network daemon, leave INPUT_FILE empty and set, for
example, EXTRA_ARGS="\-\-listen udp:514 \-\-send tcp://siem:514".
.TP
.I /etc/syslogcef/conf.d/*.conf
Per\-instance configuration for the
.I syslogcef@.service
template unit. Each file defines one independent pipeline (its own
input, output, mapping, and restart policy); start one with
\fBsystemctl enable \-\-now syslogcef@\fIname\fR, where the instance
name selects the matching conf.d file. A commented sample is installed
as \fIexample.conf.sample\fR. On Alpine, create an OpenRC instance by
symlinking \fI/etc/init.d/syslogcef\fR to \fIsyslogcef.name\fR with its
own \fI/etc/conf.d/syslogcef.name\fR.
.TP
.I /etc/logrotate.d/syslogcef
Rotates flat \fI.cef\fR archives under \fI/var/log/syslogcef\fR daily
using copytruncate (the service keeps its output open). Outputs using
strftime templates rotate themselves and are not matched.
.TP
.I syslogcef.service
systemd unit (RPM and Debian packages). By default the service runs as
root, which is what allows it to read arbitrary log files. The unit
grants CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE so that administrators who drop privileges
(by setting
.B User=
in an override, with a group able to read the input logs) can still
bind ports below 1024. On Alpine the equivalent OpenRC service is
configured via
.IR /etc/conf.d/syslogcef .
.TP
.I syslogcef.mappings
Bundled mapping files, importable from the Python package
(\fBfrom syslogcef.mappings import load_mapping\fR).
.SH SECURITY
All log content is treated as untrusted. Pipes and backslashes are
escaped in CEF header fields (CR/LF stripped) and pipes, equals signs,
backslashes, and CR/LF are escaped in extension values, preventing
header forgery and record splitting. The listener decodes input as
UTF-8 with replacement and never evaluates message content. Report
vulnerabilities privately; see the SECURITY.md file in the source
distribution.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rsyslogd (8),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR logger (1)
